The concept-audit Examples entry is where the next agent learns the method,
so a retracted argument left standing there is worse than a stale field name.
Three changes, all to the 2026-09-17 entry.

1. RENAME NOTE. The skip-host field is now silence_reason (schema 0.20.10),
   which was this audit's own verdict A. The entry keeps the old name
   throughout because that is what was measured, and says so.

2. RETRACTION. The entry argued WI-gidid re-frames the ADR-0056 item 5
   decision -- not 'should we EMIT an AnalysisRun for a pass that never ran'
   but 'should we KEEP the one we already made', a materially weaker case for
   the ABSENT != EMPTY objection. That argument was withdrawn on evidence:
   the discarded record was dumped and is near-vacuous. Every non-default
   field is already in the skip entry, and toolchain is bare PRECISELY
   because the grammar it would report is the missing thing. WI-gidid
   downgraded P2 -> P3.

3. OUTCOME, with the two transferable lessons, both about what an audit
   cannot see:

   - It measured ONE repo at DEFAULT settings, so it could not see a pass
     landing in NEITHER host. That third outcome only opens behind an opt-in
     flag, and no CI run or bakeoff sets one. Name CONFIGURATION coverage
     beside corpus coverage when saying what a measurement did not reach.

   - It audited where the silence is REPORTED and never asked whether the
     dispatcher's input to that decision was sound. It was not: INV-homur,
     six analyzers read extensions the profile does not count, so passes
     were skipped as no_candidate_files having never looked (359 -> 367
     symbols on one 19-file repo). An audit of a field should also audit the
     predicate that decides what that field gets to say.

